[Unit]
Description=CALYPSO daily correctness + governance checks. Runs the fleet correctness audit (GATING — a P&L identity break fails this unit and publishes a DATA_QUALITY alert) and reports the measurement clock, the 15-clean-trading-day gate that decides when real money may start. Read-only: it opens every database mode=ro and imports neither IBClient nor BrokerClient, so it cannot place, cancel or modify an order. The one thing it writes is SQLite's own -shm sidecar, which ANY reader of a WAL database must be able to create. It exists because both checks previously depended on someone remembering to run a script — which is how three telemetry columns sat empty for ten weeks and were found by hand on 2026-09-30, and how the go-live gate stayed prose that nobody could compute.
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=calypso
Group=calypso
WorkingDirectory=/opt/calypso

# ORDER MATTERS, and the first version of this file had it wrong. A Type=oneshot
# unit stops at the first ExecStart that fails, so with the GATING audit first
# the clock report never ran on exactly the days the audit found something —
# i.e. it went silent precisely when the most was going on. Verified by running
# it: the audit exited 1, the unit failed, and the clock never printed.
#
# 1. Governance FIRST, and non-gating (`-`). It always runs, and a clock
#    failure can never be mistaken for a correctness break. Prints BOTH the
#    strict streak and the streak that honours self-granted exemption trailers;
#    when they disagree, someone exempted their own change and the report names
#    the commit.
ExecStart=-/opt/calypso/.venv/bin/python -m scripts.measurement_clock

# 2. Correctness LAST, and GATING: exits non-zero on any I1-I5 identity break,
#    so the unit shows in `systemctl --failed`, and --alert pushes it out,
#    because a failed unit nobody runs `systemctl --failed` against is not a
#    detection. Being last means its exit code is still the unit's exit code.
ExecStart=-/opt/calypso/.venv/bin/python -m scripts.audit_telemetry_health --alert
ExecStart=/opt/calypso/.venv/bin/python -m scripts.audit_fleet_correctness --alert

StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

# Read-only sandbox, with ONE exception, added 2026-11-16 after measuring that
# this unit had crashed on 8 of its last 13 runs (10-31, 11-03..06, 11-11,
# 11-13, 11-14) with
#
#     sqlite3.OperationalError: unable to open database file
#
# and that each crash presented as the unit's gating exit 1 — i.e. "a P&L
# identity break" — for a fleet whose identities were in fact all holding.
#
# WHY A WRITE IS NEEDED TO READ. Every variant database is WAL-mode. A
# read-only SQLite connection to a WAL database still has to open the `-shm`
# sidecar, and when the last writer closes cleanly SQLite DELETES `-wal`/`-shm`
# — so a reader arriving after that has to CREATE the shm. Under
# ProtectSystem=strict the whole of /opt/calypso is read-only, so it could not,
# and the audit died. `mode=ro` still forbids writing the databases themselves,
# which is the guarantee that actually matters; this grants only the sidecar.
#
# The scripts ALSO handle the failure now (an unreadable database reports
# UNAUDITABLE and exits 2, distinct from a break's 1, and no longer aborts the
# remaining variants). That belt stays: this line makes the audit actually RUN,
# the code change makes a future failure honest instead of silent. Neither one
# substitutes for the other.
ReadWritePaths=/opt/calypso/data

NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
PrivateDevices=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
